REM Group 1 -- reported reproduction #475a: a byte-range IOR reassignment on one comma/semicolon
REM joined line. The closing statement's hover must name the byte-range reason, never guess.
a$=OPTS; A$(1,1)=IOR(A$(1,1),$C2$); SETOPTS A$

REM Group 2 -- an absolute hex literal. Decodes directly from the string, no chain walk involved.
SETOPTS $00C20240000000000000000000000000$

REM Group 3 -- reported reproduction #475b: a byte-range AND reassignment across separate LET
REM statements. Same unsafe byte-range reason as group 1, over separate physical lines.
LET A$=OPTS
LET A$(2,1)=AND(A$(2,1),$7F$)
SETOPTS A$

REM Group 4 -- canonical statically-safe whole-variable chain, over a different tracked variable
REM than groups 1 and 3. Proves the editable decode path still works end-to-end.
B$=OPTS
B$=IOR(B$,$01$)
B$=AND(B$,$FE$)
SETOPTS B$
